<tt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml" xmlns:tts="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml#styling" xml:lang="en"><head><layout xmlns:tts="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml#styling"><region xml:id="r1"><style tts:origin="0% 85%"/><style tts:extent="100% 15%"/><style tts:opacity=".85"/><style tts:fontSize="18px"/><style tts:backgroundColor="black"/><style tts:color="#FFD056"/><style tts:textAlign="center"/><style tts:displayAlign="after"/><style tts:padding="10% 5%"/><style tts:wrapOption="wrap"/></region></layout><styling xmlns:tts="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml#styling"><style id="s0" tts:color="#FFD056" tts:fontSize="95%" tts:textAlign="center" tts:wrapOption="wrap%"/></styling></head><body><div style="s0"><p begin="00:00:03.10" end="00:00:05.9">Welcome to mit tom.</p><p begin="00:00:05.9" end="00:00:06.8">My name is Dr.</p><p begin="00:00:06.8" end="00:00:07.4">hassle.</p><p begin="00:00:08.5" end="00:00:13.8">I am honored to give you this introduction<br/>to clinical nutrition lecture today.</p><p begin="00:00:13.8" end="00:00:19.8">A little bit about me before I began,<br/>i am an associate professor here at n Y p</p><p begin="00:00:19.8" end="00:00:23.5">in New York in the Department<br/>of Family medicine.</p><p begin="00:00:24.8" end="00:00:28.1">I'm also a board certified<br/>family medicine,</p><p begin="00:00:28.1" end="00:00:33.5">physician with osteopathic manipulation,<br/>as well as a diplomat</p><p begin="00:00:33.5" end="00:00:38.1">of the American board of<br/>obesity medicine here at n Y T.</p><p begin="00:00:38.1" end="00:00:40.8">com.<br/>I also serve in the role as director of</p><p begin="00:00:40.8" end="00:00:42.9">inter professional education.</p><p begin="00:00:42.9" end="00:00:50.2">So it's a nice mix taken care half of<br/>my time, and then I'll teach thing.</p><p begin="00:00:50.2" end="00:00:58.7">And then a little service back to and<br/>I'm also a graduate.</p><p begin="00:00:58.7" end="00:00:59.4">We were night.</p><p begin="00:00:59.4" end="00:01:01.4">com and I graduated in 2008.</p><p begin="00:01:02.6" end="00:01:05.3">And let's get started.</p><p begin="00:01:05.3" end="00:01:08.8">This is something that has been<br/>a passionate mine to integrate</p><p begin="00:01:08.8" end="00:01:11.8">into medical education</p><p begin="00:01:11.8" end="00:01:16.3">because it was something that was not<br/>provided to us when we were students.</p><p begin="00:01:16.3" end="00:01:19.7">And when you become a resident and<br/>then and</p><p begin="00:01:19.7" end="00:01:24.6">attending the patient to you,<br/>to be able to Coach them.</p><p begin="00:01:24.6" end="00:01:28.9">And teach them about what a balance<br/>nutritional plan looks like.</p><p begin="00:01:35.8" end="00:01:38.10">So this is a picture of me back in 2010.</p><p begin="00:01:38.10" end="00:01:44.2">I was a resident at good<br/>samaritan hospital in West Islip,</p><p begin="00:01:44.2" end="00:01:46.6">and that's my colleague in the background,<br/>amy.</p><p begin="00:01:48.3" end="00:01:52.2">So this clinical nutrition,</p><p begin="00:01:52.2" end="00:01:57.10">physical activity, my additional board<br/>certification and obesity medicine is</p><p begin="00:01:57.10" end="00:02:02.5">not just a professional passion of mine,<br/>but it's also a personal passion of mine.</p><p begin="00:02:04.4" end="00:02:09.6">So these are the session session<br/>objectives for today's lecture.</p><p begin="00:02:09.6" end="00:02:14.6">And they can be found on your a, C,<br/>P g which is your class preparation guide.</p><p begin="00:02:14.6" end="00:02:18.2">And that should all be on the web for you.</p><p begin="00:02:20.8" end="00:02:24.4">So me, tricia,<br/>how are actually closely related</p><p begin="00:02:28.2" end="00:02:33.6">More than half of all American<br/>adults have one or more preventable</p><p begin="00:02:33.6" end="00:02:38.6">chronic diseases which can be related<br/>to either for eating habits or</p><p begin="00:02:38.6" end="00:02:43.10">patterns or physical activity patterns or<br/>a combination of both.</p><p begin="00:02:47.3" end="00:02:52.4">So I'm going to give you a lot of<br/>wine from the dietary guidelines or</p><p begin="00:02:52.4" end="00:02:57.8">American which is recently<br/>updated in the end of 2020.</p><p begin="00:02:59.1" end="00:03:03">So they usually for 5 years,<br/>based off of evidence based medicine</p><p begin="00:03:03" end="00:03:05.7">is all the research that<br/>they're doing every 5 years.</p><p begin="00:03:05.7" end="00:03:11.5">They try to update the guidelines<br/>based on the newest research.</p><p begin="00:03:11.5" end="00:03:16.7">So looking at this table or<br/>figure, you'll see like the 2</p><p begin="00:03:16.7" end="00:03:23.6">dietary guidelines over<br/>time From 2000 bond, 2006.</p><p begin="00:03:23.6" end="00:03:28.4">When they be updating more and<br/>more the guidelines to now</p><p begin="00:03:28.4" end="00:03:32.1">pretty much about the same<br/>somewhere between like 56 percent,</p><p begin="00:03:32.1" end="00:03:37.7">60 percent of healing and<br/>these dietary guidelines are really for</p><p begin="00:03:37.7" end="00:03:41.6">patients that don't have medical<br/>problems initially or neat.</p><p begin="00:03:41.6" end="00:03:46.1">So the aiming guidelines really to<br/>promote help and prevent disease, but</p><p begin="00:03:46.1" end="00:03:49.5">diet related chronic diseases<br/>such as cardiovascular disease,</p><p begin="00:03:49.5" end="00:03:53.2">diabetes, obesity, and<br/>even some cancer types.</p><p begin="00:03:53.2" end="00:03:57.9">They pull the major public Health problems<br/>in the United States as well as the world</p><p begin="00:04:02.9" end="00:04:09.2">So this figure info graphic show<br/>is across like the lifespan,</p><p begin="00:04:09.2" end="00:04:13.4">you know, who is it hearing<br/>to these dietary guidelines.</p><p begin="00:04:13.4" end="00:04:17.9">So if you look at very interesting<br/>that ages $2.00 to $4.00,</p><p begin="0